Everest conquered on May 29th 1953  Edmund Hillary and Sherpa Tenzing Norgay  made history as the first climbers to  reach the summit of Mount Everest the  world's tallest mountain two unassuming  man had climbed the 29,000 foot monarch  of the Himalayas in England present-day  runner John Griffin has had plenty of  free time during his country's lockdown  and his three-story home has plenty of  stairs which planted a thought in his  head my idea the other day and I just  walked to the top of my house to my  bedroom for the fifth time and I said to  my wife I think by the time this crisis  is over I will have climbed Everest  inspired the 53 year old decided to do  it climb the equivalent of the height of  Mount Everest an amazing 29,035 feet one  step at a time not just for the  challenge and the exercise but also to  raise money for UK food banks earlier  this week he began his ascent with the  help of a computer program to track his  progress at the halfway point he  celebrated in typical British fashion  finally four days 29 hours and 41  thousand steps later  Griffin reached the summit of his own  imaginary Mountain greeted by his family  on a rooftop terrace a worthy substitute  for what climbers call the roof of the  world so getting exercise getting people  who need its food and putting smiles on  people's faces not bad Dana I'm glad he  did it and not me that's all I'll say
 

